1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,720
 Welcome to Building a Gentic Rag with Llama Index.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:06,880
 I'm joined by Jerry Liu, who is co-founder and CEO of

3
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,280
 Llama Index and the instructor for this course.

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:11,760
 Thanks, Andrew. I'm super excited to be here with you.

5
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:14,360
 In this course, you'll learn about a Gentic Rag,

6
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:17,800
 a framework to help you build research agents capable of

7
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:19,560
 advanced tool use, reasoning,

8
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:21,880
 and decision-making over your data.

9
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:25,640
 For example, what if you have a set of research papers on some topic,

10
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:29,320
 and you want to pull out the parts relevant to a question you want to ask,

11
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:31,680
 and you get a synthesis of what the papers say.

12
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:35,760
 This is a complex request that require multiple steps of processing.

13
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,320
 Further, earlier steps of processing,

14
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:41,080
 like maybe identifying a theme from one paper,

15
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:43,520
 might also change the later steps that are needed,

16
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:47,360
 like retrieving additional information from other papers about that theme.

17
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:51,640
 In comparison, the standard rag pipeline, which is very popular,

18
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:55,720
 is mostly good for simpler questions over a small set of documents,

19
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:59,520
 and works by retrieving some context, sticking that into an OM prompt,

20
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:03,080
 and then just calling the OM a single time to get a response.

21
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:07,280
 This course will take the idea of chatting over your data to the next level,

22
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,120
 and show you how to build an autonomous research agent.

23
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:16,160
 You'll learn a progression of reasoning ingredients to a building of four agents.

24
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:21,680
 First, routing, where you add decision-making to route requests to multiple tools.

25
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:26,560
 Next, tool use, where you create an interface for agents to select a tool,

26
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:29,480
 as well as generate the right arguments for that tool.

27
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:32,800
 And then finally, multi-step reasoning with tool use,

28
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:37,080
 where you use an OM to perform multi-step reasoning with a range of tools,

29
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:40,120
 while retaining memory throughout that process.

30
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:42,760
 You'll learn how to effectively interact with an agent,

31
00:01:42,760 --> 00:01:46,280
 and use its capabilities for detailed control and oversight.

32
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:49,760
 This will allow you to not only create a higher-level research assistant

33
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:54,680
 over your RAG pipelines, but also give you more effective ways to guide its actions.

34
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:58,760
 Specifically, you'll learn how to ensure debuggability of the LLN.

35
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:01,360
 We'll look at how to step through what your agent is doing,

36
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,080
 and how to use that to improve your agent.

37
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:06,760
 One additional very powerful tool is to let the user

38
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:09,960
 optionally inject guidance at intermediate steps.

39
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:13,160
 For example, if you see it searching the wrong document,

40
00:02:13,160 --> 00:02:16,960
 a little nudge to search a different document from human input,

41
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,760
 much like an experienced manager, that's you,

42
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:23,960
 giving a more junior employee a nudge to consider a new piece of information,

43
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:25,760
 can give much better performance.

44
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:28,560
 Many people have worked to create this course.

45
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:34,160
 I'd like to thank, from Llama Index, Logan Markowicz, and Andrea Fajardo.

46
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:38,960
 From dblm.ai, Dialla Ezzedine also contributed to this course.

47
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:42,560
 In the first lesson, you will build a router over a single document

48
00:02:42,560 --> 00:02:45,960
 that can handle both question answering as well as summarization.

49
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:49,560
 That sounds great. Let's go on to the next video and get started.

